#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106588} :activity/id #uuid "2af93617-2850-5d80-8e89-a45566d1d99b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049223 :topic/id #uuid "e4ba6b67-ce78-5f37-b120-6055b0e97c6c" :topic/math-academy-id 1319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107137 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute $n={\\color{blue}3}$ into the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ,$ which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "8edf1574-04d8-5aea-839f-9c03c16e6ab5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6654 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6daf0d6e-d3ca-5943-93ac-6a8d0ae04d45" :step/math-academy-id 136665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107138 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has $n={\\color{blue}4}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1de6f70b-9797-56cc-b325-b5375efaf06b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6688 :tutorial/title "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "55ff9455-d9c5-5dfd-b9aa-7d7ad79f0ad7" :step/math-academy-id 136666 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105440 :question/id #uuid "757aacb7-c988-5f1f-9ae6-c69ebf1399ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-10772"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d6d5827-91a8-5fd4-85ea-7564e8f5396e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500366}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "93903594-40c4-5f1f-b805-5569c124dd4b" :step/math-academy-id 136667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107139 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has $n={\\color{blue}5}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." :tutorial/id #uuid "573fd3ba-e480-5a75-ba71-1fd15e977637" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6738 :tutorial/title "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Pentagon"} :step/id #uuid "9d73fd08-d0b3-549f-8356-26bd528a081c" :step/math-academy-id 136670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105438 :question/id #uuid "5f89d47f-4068-508e-9fdd-1f3768d53a92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad93a16-17d4-50ff-89c8-f3d966df5f1a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571431}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Quantities in Quadrilaterals and Pentagons"} :step/id #uuid "66ba6652-6516-5c96-9fc2-f1a279bbcdce" :step/math-academy-id 136671 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105439 :question/id #uuid "7d0dd769-07d8-58c6-a2c8-d6165ca5bb5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b111120-41dd-5a58-bcc5-b0b6fc78b384" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048354}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571389}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Sides of a Polygon Given the Sum of Its Interior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "7f2b2f6c-0f00-518c-8edf-15031fffc70c" :step/math-academy-id 136674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107170 :tutorial/content "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles. \n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ,$ and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "211863f5-73c2-57af-83e9-696d21971d5b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9114 :tutorial/title "Justification of the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "19ab5e9d-cd9a-5abe-b2d8-a8c2a4b0511a" :step/math-academy-id 136677}] :activity/title "Interior Angles of Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105973}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549143} :activity/id #uuid "44b9f3a5-0dff-53bd-9ea1-9ffa89f58594" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049223 :topic/id #uuid "e4ba6b67-ce78-5f37-b120-6055b0e97c6c" :topic/math-academy-id 1319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f8c4f4d-a7cb-5f36-ba29-a840d9bbe587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548564} {:answer/id #uuid "ae31482c-97e7-5e10-8bf9-1fb019613501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548700} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5c0925-8ebb-5ac7-b69a-43148347d1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548752} {:answer/id #uuid "ef70b68e-e007-52ca-93c9-efe7e0c22ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548828} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c3f901-4de5-5726-95e7-07fe74a05efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548752} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f0acf3-60c9-5fe9-a760-cefa37eb9b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "918f9202-d934-535a-b14f-29131ec63f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118069" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${3960}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=3\\,{960}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =3\\,{960}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{3\\,{960}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =22 \\\\ n & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=24$ sides."} :step/id #uuid "607ff265-5b9e-5bf6-aea9-88e5319e920b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23ba891b-8c25-5525-b032-ab5b189de223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548425} {:answer/id #uuid "779f08b8-d128-5006-babf-2a4ce92fda54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548574} {:answer/id #uuid "78305b75-af32-5483-8ec7-da87251e1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{14}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548577} {:answer/id #uuid "8845496d-a3e1-5e36-8bf1-e36d44baa191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{10}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548617} {:answer/id #uuid "c14baac9-826a-5be1-a8d3-292463298b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548574} :answer-field/id #uuid "172b1f55-032f-506f-a1f3-de37620daea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d216a5f-ca4b-530d-81c3-76f09a214af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-4316" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa797de4c7edfb4e4c4bc4eab40e8443f832c0407f08fd9d9ae28730c468a71e.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $OMNP$ shown above, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠O+m∠M+m∠N+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {93}^{∘}+(14x-{7}^{∘})+(11x-{2}^{∘})+{76}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 25x+{160}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 25x & ={200}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={8}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "85a2f551-1e0a-5802-976a-5d521b297245" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc54541-63d3-5382-8d61-ad8fb7a2df0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548386} {:answer/id #uuid "55f686c3-4dc7-5ee6-94df-392ace9f44b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548516} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ab1399-f698-5779-856e-01b204aa3767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548680} {:answer/id #uuid "b20abdd4-a291-58d8-b13c-b588f3c268d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{34}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548708} {:answer/id #uuid "b25b8a4a-c870-5c94-9647-8973446f4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{68}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548516} :answer-field/id #uuid "c276b696-0a88-550f-b1ad-bf6a66980d06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1f1caf2-8ee2-5aae-ae77-233643724815" :question/math-academy-id "q-4231" :question/problem "![](images/48/48d9d8513f45b87f9deffeffcef31e49a6dcaba0b033c91a918c1d72d87eb215.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ above, determine the measure of $∠C.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral  must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x+{90}^{∘}+3x+6x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{90}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={270}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =3x \\\\  & =3({27}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={81}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ceeba030-1335-5012-a7a2-5d16d0992cf8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549120}}] :activity/title "Interior Angles of Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548341}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571490} :activity/id #uuid "138360b1-768e-5e3c-ac72-e07110620982" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049223 :topic/id #uuid "e4ba6b67-ce78-5f37-b120-6055b0e97c6c" :topic/math-academy-id 1319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "087ccede-33f5-5195-9b20-20231b7111e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570904} {:answer/id #uuid "57b5d47e-d89a-5826-aea5-db085b14ccab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571026} {:answer/id #uuid "c47bbdb0-97dd-558f-8cbd-d5cedcbbf537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571195} {:answer/id #uuid "da9be92d-36c8-5e75-9fa1-ce0bcc251023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571227} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd6fbd6-2ac9-570c-8cf7-38cf4ec08434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571227} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5a75a9-cf7a-5ed4-8499-ccb893baaf9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "407e8da8-449d-50a1-846d-17321f5d621f" :question/math-academy-id "q-68108" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${1260}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=1\\,{260}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =1\\,{260}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{1\\,{260}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =7 \\\\ n & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=9$ sides (so it is a nonagon)."} :step/id #uuid "000349e2-1e18-52dc-948a-61a295c5ca01"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55bbde82-dc2a-5198-9282-78920ca4d7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571020} {:answer/id #uuid "68d9c9d2-c4ee-5369-ad28-406b236d5756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{80}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571055} {:answer/id #uuid "715bae87-ddc1-5d3b-a222-2343cb0987a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{72}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571067} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecfa468-92a9-55f5-9f15-5d0ce9e5f16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{84}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571113} {:answer/id #uuid "cee2fb05-a7b0-5c6e-88e4-091526dba551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571210}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571020} :answer-field/id #uuid "f576a5f9-158f-5c8f-9257-5b6840166ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e56717a-c5ad-51b8-ae63-5cc496ab485e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4310" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc485a3c47295e08c917d1c9a2e90df0a0d6eaa931f4df23e17700da1536ca6e.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $OMNP$ shown above, find the measure of $∠N.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M+m∠N+m∠P+m∠O & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {104}^{∘}+(10x-{4}^{∘})+{104}^{∘}+{76}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{280}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={80}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={8}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠N & =10x-{4}^{∘} \\\\  & =10({8}^{∘})-{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={76}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "168198c9-67f3-5f0f-8907-74beb26be266" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "906388b6-93f9-5ca5-a23a-5c68acb32845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104039} {:answer/id #uuid "038caf9d-24e4-5261-b4b7-93f45459b83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104040} {:answer/id #uuid "210f4cc2-b4c4-5b25-9892-f0a285e15759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "115^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104041} {:answer/id #uuid "3c811a6e-5a94-5f69-8deb-0d0415c81014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "110^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104042} {:a